WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.620
This week we start a new topic, formal logic systems. The idea of this topic is
that we

00:06.620 --> 00:12.140
model the kind of logical arguments that we encounter in real life and also in
mathematics

00:12.140 --> 00:21.300
by mathematical objects. Then we can use maths to reason about arguments and
prove properties of

00:21.300 --> 00:28.800
logical reasoning. So what do we mean by a logical argument? Well, we mean
something which starts

00:28.800 --> 00:35.120
with a bunch of claims which are assumptions and then has a conclusion. Usually
we put the word

00:35.120 --> 00:41.360
therefore before a logical conclusion in an argument. Here's one that my parents
made many

00:41.360 --> 00:48.160
times. If you can watch cartoons, you're not ill. If you're not ill, you go to
school. If you go to

00:48.160 --> 00:55.040
school, you cannot watch cartoons. Therefore, you cannot watch cartoons. Now, we
may disagree

00:55.040 --> 01:00.520
with the premises of this argument. Believe me, I tried. But if we accept those
premises,

01:01.100 --> 01:07.020
then logically we must accept the conclusion. And what do I mean by logically
there? Well,

01:07.520 --> 01:14.980
these expressions contain some of the key phrases that we saw in chapter two. We
have if here

01:16.320 --> 01:25.700
and here and here. And we have not here, here and here. And again, we have a not
down here.

01:26.120 --> 01:34.400
So the assumptions express relationships between various claims by combining
these claims together

01:34.400 --> 01:41.140
using the key phrases. Now, the basic claims about the world, which we can't
break down

01:41.940 --> 01:48.580
using the key phrases, we will refer to as atomic propositions. And those have
to be things which

01:48.580 --> 01:55.560
can either be true or false, which could be asserted, denied, argued, debated.
So basically

01:55.560 --> 02:02.480
something is a claim if it would be grammatical to say they say and then that
thing. For instance,

02:02.980 --> 02:09.420
they say the moon is made of cheese. That makes grammatical sense. It may not
make sense in the

02:09.420 --> 02:14.660
world, but there's no grammatical problem with that. So the moon is made of
cheese is a claim.

02:15.020 --> 02:20.600
And that's the kind of thing that we're going to model using an atomic
proposition because

02:20.600 --> 02:26.220
it doesn't contain any of the key phrases. So what atomic propositions do we
have here?

02:26.560 --> 02:35.160
Well, we have you can watch cartoons. We have you are ill. Let's just work
around the not,

02:35.160 --> 02:40.880
which is a key phrase. Here we have you are ill again, again in the negative
form,

02:41.460 --> 02:48.640
and we have you go to school. And then we have you go to school again. And then
here we have

02:48.640 --> 02:56.600
you can watch cartoons, but it has a knot inside it. And finally, we have the
negated version of

02:56.600 --> 03:02.840
you can watch cartoons. Now, one way that we can tell that this argument is
logically correct

03:02.840 --> 03:10.480
is by just replacing each of these atomic propositions with a variable. So if I
rub these

03:10.480 --> 03:36.360
out and put a variable name in here, the argument still makes sense. So we don't
actually need to

03:36.360 --> 03:44.460
know what claims about the world P, Q and R are here, because if we accept all
of the assumptions

03:44.460 --> 03:51.440
of this argument, then we have to accept the conclusion no matter which claims
P, Q and R

03:51.440 --> 03:59.340
are. Now let's look at a real world argument, which is not logically valid. Here
it is.

03:59.760 --> 04:06.760
My assumptions are my teapot is made of glass and an anvil fell on my teapot.
Therefore,

04:07.260 --> 04:13.560
my teapot is broken. Now, you might think that the conclusion here does follow
if you accept

04:13.560 --> 04:20.280
both of the premises, but it doesn't follow just because of the meanings of the
key phrases,

04:20.500 --> 04:27.340
because if we look here, we have absolutely no key phrases. So if we were to
replace these basic

04:27.340 --> 04:35.940
propositions, these atomic propositions by variables, we would get which says if
we assume

04:35.940 --> 04:42.300
P and Q, whatever they are, then we have to accept R whatever it is. And that
doesn't work.

04:42.300 --> 04:50.700
So in logic, we study when a series of assumptions, if accepted, forces us to
accept a conclusion

04:50.700 --> 04:57.060
because of the meanings of the key phrases. Now, as I said, we want to model
these claims

04:57.060 --> 05:02.600
by mathematical objects, so that we can start proving theorems about how logic
works.

05:02.880 --> 05:10.040
So what we need to do is assuming that we have a set of symbols to refer to
atomic propositions,

05:10.040 --> 05:16.940
we have to introduce a notation that combines them for every possible key
phrase. That's what

05:16.940 --> 05:23.660
we'll do next. So here is the formal definition from the notes. If you look at
the first case,

05:23.920 --> 05:30.020
it tells us that if we have one of our propositional variables, say P, then that
by itself

05:30.020 --> 05:35.820
should count as a propositional formula. So propositional formula is going to be
our formal

05:35.820 --> 05:41.960
model of a claim. And we're saying, well, that claim could be an atomic claim
with no key phrases

05:41.960 --> 05:48.220
inside it. So we're just going to represent it by a variable. In this unit,
we'll use the letters of

05:48.220 --> 05:55.360
the alphabet from P onwards to mean single atomic propositional variables. Now
we have two expressions

05:55.360 --> 06:04.060
read bottom and top, or false and true. We'll have those as formulas. They won't
be as important

06:04.060 --> 06:11.080
this week. First, bottom is intended to represent a claim which is always false
or which you could

06:11.080 --> 06:17.340
never defend. And top, or true, is intended to represent a claim which is always
true and

06:17.340 --> 06:23.960
which therefore you could never deny. So now we have the atomic propositional
formulas.

06:24.360 --> 06:29.980
We need some ways to build more complicated propositional formulas out of them.
Just like

06:29.980 --> 06:35.980
the example, our atomic propositions were connected by the key phrases and that
allowed

06:35.980 --> 06:42.700
us to reason about them logically. So if we look at the third case here, it says
if A is a propositional

06:42.700 --> 06:48.680
formula, then this funny expression is a propositional formula. And we will read
that

06:48.680 --> 06:55.700
not A. Notice that we use the early letters in the alphabet, A, B, C, etc, for
propositional

06:55.700 --> 07:00.380
formulas in this unit. And it's important to remember that these are very
different things.

07:00.780 --> 07:07.160
Propositional variables are single symbols, but as we've seen in the third case,
a propositional

07:07.160 --> 07:13.840
formula is a string of symbols. So we will read the one in the third case as not
A and our intended

07:13.840 --> 07:20.780
meaning is that it means A is false. Now in the last case of the definition
here, we assume that

07:20.780 --> 07:26.860
constructed two propositional formulas A and B. And because those are formulas,
they could be very

07:26.860 --> 07:33.380
long expressions full of lots of symbols. Then the following are also
propositional formulas.

07:33.920 --> 07:41.800
The first one we read A and B. The second one we read A or B. And the third one
we read A implies

07:41.800 --> 07:50.200
B or if A then B. So those are going to be strings of symbols which we intend to
model

07:50.200 --> 07:58.700
the ideas of the key phrases and, or, and implies. But at the moment, they're
just symbols. We haven't

07:58.700 --> 08:04.500
yet put anything into the model that says what these symbols actually mean.
We've just defined

08:04.500 --> 08:09.240
them and I've given you a way to read them intuitively, which suggests the rules
that we're

08:09.240 --> 08:17.720
going to define later. Finally, in mathematics, we often have conditions where
we say if and only if.

08:17.720 --> 08:25.340
So it's very useful to have a shorthand notation for that. And for that we use
this double arrow,

08:25.440 --> 08:32.020
which we read double implication by implication, or if and only if. But we
consider that just to

08:32.020 --> 08:40.080
be a shorthand for this longer formula where we spell out what it means
precisely. So let's see

08:40.080 --> 08:48.860
an example of a logical formula. Well, to get started, I'm just going to pick
some propositional

08:48.860 --> 08:54.820
variables. So P is a propositional variable, so that's a formula. Q is a
propositional variable,

08:54.820 --> 09:01.360
so that's a formula by itself. Now P space Q is not a formula because there are
no cases of the

09:01.360 --> 09:06.320
definition which would allow us to just put two variables right next to each
other. But we could

09:06.320 --> 09:13.020
join them with the or symbol, for example. So P or Q is a propositional formula.
And strictly

09:13.020 --> 09:19.700
speaking, I should bracket this because the definition asks us to bracket it.
Now I could

09:19.700 --> 09:26.760
use the third case to make this a bit more complicated and say not P or Q. And
then I could

09:26.760 --> 09:33.900
go back to the fourth case and, for instance, say not P or Q implies P. That's a
propositional

09:33.900 --> 09:42.460
formula. Or not P or Q implies not P. That's a propositional formula. So
anything that we can get

09:42.460 --> 09:49.740
starting from propositional variables, P, Q, R, etc., and combining them using
the cases of this

09:49.740 --> 09:56.240
definition is a propositional formula. Now because of the brackets in the
definition,

09:57.120 --> 10:03.760
propositional formulas can get very cumbersome. Look at the example here. P and
Q implies Q or

10:03.760 --> 10:10.800
P. There are too many brackets in that and they actually stop us from reading it
easily. So we're

10:10.800 --> 10:16.780
going to define a convention which allows us not to write down the brackets even
though, strictly

10:16.780 --> 10:23.460
speaking, in the formula as defined in the real mathematical object, those
brackets are actually

10:23.460 --> 10:28.780
there. We're just not going to write them as human beings when we want to
describe which formula we

10:28.780 --> 10:35.560
So the idea is that we rank the different connectives. So we say that negation
binds

10:35.560 --> 10:41.040
the most tightly. So when we're thinking about where to put brackets, if we have
a negation,

10:41.200 --> 10:47.640
that will be deepest inside all of the brackets. Next come and and or, which are
equal to each other,

10:47.700 --> 10:53.920
but they bind a bit less tightly than negation. And finally, implication and by
implication.

10:53.920 --> 10:58.900
So that means that if we have a formula with, for example, an and and an
implication,

10:59.620 --> 11:04.160
the and will be within the brackets and the implication will be on the outside.

11:04.680 --> 11:10.720
It's a bit easier to think about this by looking at examples. So for instance,
we can just write

11:11.360 --> 11:19.560
P and Q implies R for this thing here with lots of brackets because our
precedence convention

11:19.560 --> 11:26.780
tells us that the and binds more tightly than the implies. So the P and Q is
deepest inside

11:26.780 --> 11:32.960
the brackets and then the implies R is on the outside. Now let's look at this
expression.

11:33.520 --> 11:39.120
We have a not here, which will bind most tightly. Now we have an implies, but
over here we have an

11:39.120 --> 11:44.540
or which binds more tightly than implies. So we should bracket this part and
then finally bracket

11:44.540 --> 11:55.500
whole thing. So in the end we get not P implies Q or R, like that. Now what
about this one,

11:55.580 --> 12:05.520
P and Q implies Q or not P? Well, the not P will be deepest inside the brackets.
Then we'll have the

12:05.520 --> 12:13.520
P and Q and the Q or not P bracketed like that. And finally, the implication,
which binds least

12:13.520 --> 12:20.160
tightly, will be the outermost connective. Now what about the expression on the
bottom? Well,

12:20.220 --> 12:25.940
remember that and and or have equal precedence. So that means that this
expression on the bottom

12:25.940 --> 12:32.080
is ambiguous and we will never write this. We either have to bracket the P and Q
or we have to

12:32.080 --> 12:39.560
bracket the Q or R so that we say unambiguously what we mean. Now because we
want to do maths

12:39.560 --> 12:44.500
with formulas, we need some definitions that let us take formulas apart. In
particular,

12:44.900 --> 12:51.280
we need to define the notion of a subformula. And the idea is that a subformula
is just any string

12:51.280 --> 12:56.420
of the formula that is itself a propositional formula. So a propositional
formula, remember,

12:56.600 --> 13:02.400
is just a mathematical object. It's just a string of symbols. And so we can take
any substring of

13:02.400 --> 13:08.600
that string and if that substring is a propositional formula, then it's a
subformula of our original

13:08.600 --> 13:15.160
formula. But we have to remember one thing first. If we write a propositional
formula without

13:15.160 --> 13:22.060
brackets, then really we mean the fully bracketed version of that propositional
formula. So before

13:22.060 --> 13:29.760
working out the subformulas, it's a good idea to put the brackets back in. Now
we can see that the

13:29.760 --> 13:45.200
subformulas are not P, Q or not P, P and Q, and the whole thing P and Q implies
Q or not P.

13:45.860 --> 13:52.820
Notice that the whole formula is a subformula of itself. Now I've missed out two
subformulas.

13:52.820 --> 14:00.400
Can you see what they are? Yes, P and Q individually are formulas, and they are
also

14:00.400 --> 14:06.380
therefore subformulas of this formula. So now I've listed all of the
subformulas,

14:06.560 --> 14:12.480
and because we took the trouble to completely bracket this formula before
looking for substrings

14:12.480 --> 14:18.620
inside it which are formulas, we didn't make the mistake of thinking that Q
implies Q, for instance,

14:18.620 --> 14:24.060
was a subformula of this formula. If we didn't put the brackets in, it would
look like it was

14:24.060 --> 14:30.740
a substring which is itself a formula, but it's clear once we put the brackets
in that Q implies

14:30.740 --> 14:37.920
open bracket Q is not a well-formed formula. So now we've modelled most of what
we saw in

14:37.920 --> 14:44.100
our intuitive example, but we haven't yet modelled the list of assumptions
followed by therefore,

14:44.100 --> 14:48.200
followed by a consequent that we saw in those arguments that we're trying to
model.

14:48.380 --> 14:54.280
So we'll call such a thing a judgment, and this is the notation that we'll use
for it.

14:54.440 --> 15:00.440
We'll write the list of assumptions separated by commas, and then this sideways
T, sometimes called

15:00.440 --> 15:05.780
the turnstile, if you want to give it a name, and then a single formula on the
right-hand side.

15:05.980 --> 15:10.620
So the list of formulas on the left we call assumptions, and the single formula
on the right

15:10.620 --> 15:16.340
we call the consequent, or sometimes conclusion. Now I say a list of
assumptions, but one important

15:16.340 --> 15:20.900
thing is that we are not going to care about the order of the assumptions in
that list.

15:21.180 --> 15:26.020
So although we write this as a list, you could think of it as a set of
assumptions where we

15:26.020 --> 15:30.420
just haven't written the curly brackets. So now we have all the ingredients that
we need

15:30.420 --> 15:35.720
to translate the intuitive example into a formal mathematical object.

15:37.940 --> 15:45.440
So here's our intuitive example, and now we can replace the first line with a
propositional formula,

15:52.290 --> 16:10.360
and the same with the second line, and the third assumption, and the conclusion.

16:15.290 --> 16:23.050
Now we can combine the three assumptions into a list of assumptions by just
putting commas between

16:23.050 --> 16:32.460
them, and we can replace the word therefore with our sideways T symbol, and now
we have

16:32.460 --> 16:38.080
our mathematical object which is intended to be a model of the meaning of that
argument.

16:39.340 --> 16:45.600
Well this is all well and good, but at the moment all we've defined is a way of
translating

16:46.120 --> 16:53.120
the terminology in the argument into a string of symbols. We haven't yet said
anything about

16:53.120 --> 16:58.660
what the strings of symbols mean. Well the way we're going to define the meaning
of these strings

16:58.660 --> 17:05.420
of symbols is by giving rules for proving that arguments in this symbolic form
are valid.

